  <section id="claims">
    <h2>The question is broader than detection: presence, horizontal location, shape, relative vertical geometry and absolute depth are scored apart</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      Does one complex SAR acquisition hold reproducible information about what is actually underground, where it is or its
      shape, that survives controls and predicts withheld ground, even if its depth stays uncertain? A method that drew a
      branching tunnel or a room’s layout at one of two possible depths would be a major success, so an uncalibrated or
      aliased depth scale does not by itself dismiss a method. Each claim is judged on its own evidence.
    </p>
    <table class="data compact">
      <thead><tr><th>claim</th><th>what bounds or tests it</th><th>where it stands</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Presence: a room here or not</td><td>the three layers below, which bound every processor within the model</td><td>under the ground’s own trembling, no useful detection ({pctp(micro.tpr_upper_at_fpr_005, 2)} found at 5% false alarms, at most, for the bench room); with a lorry beside a room under a 5 m roof, one image holds at most {tvTxt(bm.strong['presence (room)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)} (an L tunnel {tvTxt(bm.strong['presence (L tunnel)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)}); only a detector told the reflectivity could reach 95% at 5%, above {Math.round(bm.strong['presence (room)'].oracle_snr_db_for_target ?? 0)} dB (the benchmark)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Horizontal location</td><td>the same bounds: telling a room here from a room there is a test between two worlds, and TV(A, B) ≤ TV(A, none) + TV(none, B)</td><td>bounded wherever presence is; in the benchmark, the room or the same room 6 m east, with a lorry beside it: one image holds at most {tvTxt(bm.strong['location (6 m)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)}, so any estimate’s mean error over the pair is at least {(3 * (1 - bm.strong['location (6 m)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)).toFixed(1)} m of the 3 m that guessing leaves; in the shape test (P2-31, below), where the data hold a layout (amplified to 20 rad) a reference detector told the shaking places it {m1(stPos?.reference.median_location_error_m)} from its centre, but at 2 rad cannot find it by scanning; the published picture read blind centres {m1(stPos?.published_blind.median_centroid_error_m)} off even at 20 rad, the controls {m1(stNum('control_centroid_error_blind_m'))}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shape: a room, an L-shaped or a branching tunnel</td><td>the same inequality between any two layouts; the shape test (P2-31) on physically generated scenes, scored blind</td><td>bounded under the ground’s own trembling (P2-32); in the benchmark, the room or the L tunnel with a lorry beside them, one image at most {tvTxt(bm.strong['shape (room or L tunnel)'].l1[bmN].tv_upper)}; in the shape test the reference detector names {pc0(stPos?.reference.shape_accuracy)} of layouts at 20 rad and {pc0(stMid?.reference.shape_accuracy)} at 2 rad when told where to look; the published picture read blind {named(stPos?.published_blind ?? ({} as StR))} at 20 rad, chance at the real level</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Relative vertical geometry</td><td>whether deeper parts are drawn deeper, whatever the scale</td><td>not shown: the published axis is the registration’s frequency (P2-06, P2-28)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Absolute depth</td><td>the axis’s calibration, and whether different depths leave different marks</td><td>uncalibrated and aliased in the published method; physically, in this model, the mark’s shape carries depth (below), so a reader that could detect could also tell depths apart</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      A bound on detection rate − false-alarm rate is a bound at every operating point: found ≤ false alarms + TV. Under the
      regional microseism envelope that leaves no prospecting value within the model, even for ranking sites (TV ≤ {sci(micro.tv_upper)});
      where the upper bound is large, as for strong shaking close by, the argument does not exclude prospecting value short of
      95% at 5%; that is not evidence that any method has it, which only an implemented detector succeeding on independent
      tests would show. The defensible conclusion is that useful recovery is excluded under these explicitly bounded
      conditions, not that vibration-based mapping from one image can never work.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="shape">
    <h2>Telling one layout from another is bounded the same way, and moving a reconstruction up or down cannot rescue it</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      If two layouts leave nearly the same image in distribution, no algorithm can tell them apart, however it
      reconstructs. A uniformly reliable shape mapper must separate two layouts even after the depth freedom it is allowed;
      the closest admissible pair of depth variants is no easier than the pair at the same depth, so the same-depth bound
      holds for every allowance. Each layout’s imprint is the lab’s solver (statically for the ambient levels, dynamically
      for a lorry close by), and the bound is on the difference between two layouts’ imprints (P2-32).
    </p>
    <table class="data compact">
      <thead><tr><th>which of the two?</th><th>tight (microseisms)</th><th>oracle (microseisms)</th><th>oracle’s bound, a lorry beside it all pass</th><th>signal over detection’s</th></tr></thead>
