  <section id="layers">
    <h2>Three layers, one answer for the ground’s own trembling; under strong shaking the whole-image references fail, and the benchmark’s per-line certificate replaces them</h2>
    <table class="data compact">
      <thead><tr><th>shaking</th><th>level</th><th>certificate (P2-30)</th><th>tight (P2-25)</th><th>oracle (P2-29)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {layers.map((l) => (
          <tr><td>{l.what}</td><td>{l.level}</td><td>{sci(l.cert)}<br /><span class="dim">{l.certNote}</span></td><td>{l.tight === null ? '—' : sci(l.tight)}<br /><span class="dim">{l.tightNote}</span></td><td>{tvTxt(l.oracle)}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      Each entry bounds detection rate − false-alarm rate; with two equally likely cases the best accuracy exceeds a coin toss
      by half of it. The certificate and the tight bound hold within fully developed speckle and the Doppler-to-time relation
      (checked pulse by pulse, P2-25); the oracle holds for any texture with independent phases, at {orc.manifest.params.snr_db} dB
      per resolution cell (above the brightest natural ground over ICEYE’s best Dwell noise floor, {orc.manifest.params.nesz_best_db} dB).
      The ambient levels are regional, not upper limits at the pyramids. Under traffic or a lorry the ground without a room
      already moves with the source, so these rows’ certificate and tight bound (no such motion in the reference) are
      restricted surrogates, not bounds; the whole image gives no floor with the lorry’s wave in it, but one along-track
      line does, and the benchmark above bounds a single image that way. The oracle, whose mean echoes differ only by the
      added motion, stays valid, with the scattered wave carried undiminished across the scene at the worst of four sampled
      sides and the grid checks’ allowances. On small test images the best test comes within {' '}{detOver.toFixed(1)} times of the tight
      bound: these are bounds, not what a detector achieves.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="theorems">
    <h2>Five theorems, each finite; none assumes a resonance peak or a particular algorithm</h2>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">A · every processor</span>
        <h3>Processing cannot add distinguishability</h3>
        <p>For any test, detection rate − false-alarm rate ≤ TV(P₁, P₀), and for equal priors the best accuracy is ½ + TV/2. Any
        channel applied the same way under both hypotheses, a sub-aperture bank, a tomogram, a renderer, a trained network,
        contracts TV. So 95% found at 5% false alarms needs TV ≥ 0.9, and a proved TV below it excludes that target for
        every method at once.</p>
        <details><summary>Proof</summary><p>Split p₁ − p₀ into positive and negative parts of equal mass TV. A test 0 ≤ t ≤ 1
        integrates to at most TV against it; the indicator of the positive part attains it. A processor K pulls back to the test
        y ↦ E[t(K(y))], so it can do no better on K’s output.</p></details>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">B · the finite change</span>
        <h3>The exact Gaussian divergence, bounded</h3>
        <p>For proper complex Gaussians, KL = tr(C₀⁻¹C₁) − d − log det(C₀⁻¹C₁) + δm*C₀⁻¹δm. With E the whitened covariance
        change and its eigenvalues e<sub>j</sub> &gt; −1, KL = Σ(e<sub>j</sub> − log(1 + e<sub>j</sub>)) + mean term, and since
        e − log(1 + e) ≤ e²/(2 min(1 + e, 1)), KL ≤ ‖E‖<sub>F</sub>²/(2(1 − ‖E‖)). Pinsker and Bretagnolle–Huber turn KL into TV. No
        expansion in the displacement is made.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">C · a certificate from a phase envelope</span>
        <h3>The whole image given the room’s largest phase</h3>
        <p>If ‖C₀<sup>−½</sup>ΔA‖<sub>F</sub> ≤ q, ρ = 2q + q² bounds ‖E‖ exactly (E = DB* + BD* + DD*), so KL ≤ ρ²/(2(1 − ρ)) and TV ≤ min(√(KL/2), √(1 − e<sup>−KL</sup>)). A phase-only change
        obeys |e<sup>−iΦ</sup> − 1|² ≤ Φ², so q² ≤ Σ|T₀|²Φ² / λ<sub>min</sub>(C₀) ≤ m P²/λ<sub>min</sub>: the peak phase P at every one of the image’s
        {' '}{(fc.geometry.pixel_count / 1e9).toFixed(2)} billion pixels, over its {(fc.geometry.in_band_bins / 1e6).toFixed(0)} million retained bins. A background
        motion bounded the same way proves λ<sub>min</sub>(C₀) ≥ (1 − √Q<sub>bg</sub>)² = {fc.background_included.covariance_floor_proved_within_model.toFixed(4)}.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">D · the oracle</span>
        <h3>Told everything but the answer</h3>
        <p>Given the reflectivity, the background motion, the shaking and both models, the raw echoes are Gaussian with the receiver
        noise’s covariance and means that differ by the echo of the room-specific motion alone: TV = erf(√d/2) exactly, d the
        noise-normalised energy of that difference. Averaged over what the oracle was told (only independent uniform phases
        needed), TV ≤ erf(√(E d)/2); the background motion cancels; 95% at 5% needs E d ≥ {fc.requirements.oracle_energy_necessary_for_tv_090.toFixed(2)}.
        Checked against pulse-by-pulse echoes on the real dwell: {orc.raw_echo_check.summary.background_x1.mean.toFixed(3)} ± {orc.raw_echo_check.summary.background_x1.se.toFixed(3)} of the formula.
        The bound is on the average over scenes; for one scene, by Markov’s inequality, d ≤ t E d with probability at least
        1 − 1/t (the spread measured on fixed scenes is a few per cent, P2-29, P2-36). Every realisation of a random
        excitation, rather than its expectation, costs at most √(2n) in amplitude for n equally loud independent directions.
        A mixture of alternatives (any prior over rooms and layouts) is no easier to detect than its hardest member; N
        independent images raise the divergence N times, TV at most √N times.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">E · depth</span>
        <h3>Two worlds a method cannot tell apart</h3>
        <p>For two admissible worlds at depths Δ apart, any depth estimate has equal-prior mean absolute error at least
        (Δ/2)(1 − TV). One indistinguishable pair is enough to rule out reliable depth over any class that contains it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">and the tight layer</span>
        <h3>What speckle adds</h3>
        <p>For speckle, motion shared by the scene leaves the image’s distribution unchanged, so only what differs from place
        to place counts: the Fisher information F = Σ|Φ̃(f − f′; f) − Φ̃(f − f′; f′)|², with a power-series remainder ε bounding
        the rest, ‖E‖ ≤ √F + ε (P2-25). This is far tighter than the certificate, and assumes the reference covariance is the
        identity, where the certificate proves its floor with the background in it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Theorems A–E are from an independent derivation made for this lab and checked in its code (<span class="mono">sim/sarsim/finite.py</span>,
      with its tests); the tight layer is <span class="mono">sim/sarsim/information.py</span>. Background:
      {' '}<a href="https://web.stanford.edu/class/stats311/lecture-notes.pdf">Duchi’s lecture notes</a> on total variation, data processing,
      Pinsker and two-point testing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mechanism">
    <h2>What a mechanism must supply: a large enough displacement integral over the ground, whatever its pattern; smooth patterns need far more</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      Corrected on 30 September after two independent reviews. The solid curves were first described as the motion any
      mechanism must supply; they hold only for the smooth pattern computed. The requirement for every pattern is on the
      displacement integral <i>E</i> = ∫ max<sub>t</sub> |Δ<i>u</i><sub>los</sub>|² d<i>A</i> (m⁴: an integrated squared
      displacement envelope, not a mechanical energy). Under strong shaking only the oracle’s version is valid: the ground
      without a room already moves with the source, and the speckle certificate has no proved reference for that world.
    </p>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      <LineChart
        title="The least room-specific line-of-sight motion for 95% found at 5% false alarms, against footprint"
        series={[
          ...rqMain.filter((r) => r.f_hz === 0.2 || r.f_hz === 80).map((r, i) => ({ name: `${r.f_hz} Hz, smooth pattern`, x: r.exact.map((e) => e.area_half_m2), y: r.exact.map((e) => e.D_necessary_m['0.9']), color: rqColours[i === 0 ? 0 : 4], width: 1.8 })),
          { name: 'any pattern, oracle', x: anyCurve(Eor).map((c) => c.A), y: anyCurve(Eor).map((c) => c.D), color: 'var(--text)', dash: '2 3', width: 1.3 },
          { name: 'any pattern, certificate (quiet)', x: anyCurve(Emin).map((c) => c.A), y: anyCurve(Emin).map((c) => c.D), color: 'var(--muted)', dash: '4 3', width: 1.3 },
        ]}
        xLabel="footprint, m²"
        yLabel="peak displacement, m, line of sight"
        logX
        logY
        yFormat="exp"
        height={240}
        caption="Solid: exact within the model for one smooth (Gaussian) pattern (P2-27), at 0.2 and 80 Hz; 2, 5 and 20 Hz lie between them in the run (20 Hz almost on the 80 Hz line). Dotted (the oracle with receiver noise, valid under any shaking) and dashed (the certificate with Giza’s background, quiet ground only): every pattern and time history, by the displacement integral, drawn as the temporal peak displacement of a motion filling the footprint evenly (a spatial rms of temporal peaks), the same at every frequency. Below a line the bound excludes 95% at 5%; between a dotted line and a solid one only the smooth pattern is excluded. Crossing a line shows neither detectability nor usefulness."
      />
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">conditional</span>
        <h3>Phase modulation, nonlinear coupling, anything</h3>
        <p>The bounds use the full complex change of the image, phase included; no spectral peak is needed. Any mechanism,
        linear or not, reaches one image only through the motion it adds. An oracle told the reflectivity, the excitation and
        both models, with receiver noise at {rq.any_pattern?.oracle_snr_db ?? 30} dB per cell, cannot reach 95% at 5% while that
        motion’s displacement integral stays below {Eor != null ? `${sci(Eor)} m⁴` : 'the boundary'}, whatever its pattern
        or time history, and whatever motion both worlds share, which cancels from its mean echoes; below
        {' '}{Eor05 != null ? `${sci(Eor05)} m⁴` : '—'} it stays within five points of chance. Every method told less
        inherits this. Under quiet ground the speckle certificate, with Giza’s background in the reference, gives
        {' '}{Emin != null ? `${sci(Emin)} m⁴` : '—'} without assuming receiver noise.</p>
        <p>The pattern matters as well as the size. {pat02 ? `A Gaussian 4 m wide at 0.2 Hz, multiplied by a cosine of 4 m period along track, holds ${pat02.ratio_to_gaussian['cos along track, 4 m period'].toFixed(0)} times its information though nowhere larger (${pat02.ratio_to_gaussian['cos along track, 1 m period'].toFixed(0)} times for a 1 m period). The Gaussian’s information is ${sci(pat02.gaussian_share_of_envelope_bound)} of the general upper bound for its envelope, 4N⟨Φ²⟩, which is a bound, not an attainable optimum; at 80 Hz it is ${pat80 ? (100 * pat80.gaussian_share_of_envelope_bound).toFixed(0) : '—'}%.` : ''}</p>
        <p>A change of phase alone moves the ground by at most twice the motion it acts on, 2<i>a</i>. To escape this
        exclusion, under its stated amplitude and noise, such a mechanism must change the phase over more than
        {' '}<i>E</i><sub>min</sub>/(2<i>a</i>)²{phaseArea != null ? `: ${Math.round(phaseArea).toLocaleString('en-US')} m² under Giza’s regional microseisms, a disk of radius ${Math.round(Math.sqrt(phaseArea / Math.PI))} m` : ''}.
        That is necessary to escape this particular bound, not sufficient for detection. Whether a physically justified
        cavity-induced field, tails included, reaches the threshold is the table’s question below. A mechanism with gain is
        not excluded: it needs its output specified (source, footprint, amplitude and phase, losses, duration) before it can
        be tested.</p>
        <details class="calculator" data-lambda={fc.geometry.wavelength_m} data-density={fc.geometry.area_density_used}>
          <summary>Try an envelope in the certificate</summary>
          <div class="inputs">
            <label>Footprint (m²)<input name="area" type="number" value="1000" min="0.001" step="100" /></label>
            <label>Peak room-specific motion (µm)<input name="displacement" type="number" value="0.12" min="0" step="0.01" /></label>
            <label>Covariance floor<input name="floor" type="number" value="1" min="0.000001" step="0.1" /></label>
          </div>
          <p><output aria-live="polite"></output></p>
          <p class="dim">The loose certificate (Theorem C): zero-mean Gaussian texture, phase-only change, continuum area. Inputs are assumptions, not measured motion.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
    <table class="data compact">
      <thead><tr><th>the bench room’s modelled imprint</th><th>short of a smooth pattern of its peak and footprint</th><th>short of the oracle’s requirement, any pattern</th><th>short of the certificate’s (quiet ground only)</th><th>oracle’s bound on found − false alarms</th><th>outcome</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rq.imprints.map((im) => (
          <tr><td>{im.label.replace('bench room, ', '').replace(', measured', '').replace('The world', 'the world')}{im.label.includes('dynamic') ? `, ${Math.round(im.f_hz)} Hz` : ''}</td><td>{sci(im.short_by)}</td><td>{shortTxt(im.short_by_oracle)}{im.short_by_oracle_near_only != null ? <span class="dim"><br />within 39 m only: {shortTxt(im.short_by_oracle_near_only)}</span> : ''}</td><td>{im.short_by_certificate != null ? shortTxt(im.short_by_certificate) : '— (no valid reference under the lorry)'}</td><td>{tvTxt(im.oracle_tv_upper)}</td><td>{im.outcome ?? '—'}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      “Short” is how many times the imprint’s amplitude would have to grow before the bound stops excluding 95% at 5%. At every
      operating point found ≤ false alarms + the bound: below 0.05 the reader is within five points of chance; below 0.9,
      95% at 5% is excluded but weaker, possibly useful performance is not; at 0.9 or more the bound does not decide
      (unresolved), which is not a demonstrated detection. The static imprint is bounded over every strain direction and
      beyond the solver’s grid by Eshelby’s void as Okada point sources, scaled to the solver{rq.static_integral ? ` (${Math.max(...rq.static_integral.solver_over_analytic_largest).toFixed(2)} times at most inside the grid, times ${rq.static_integral.envelope_margin})` : ''},
      with the grid check’s 10% and a random field’s peak (3/√2 times its rms amplitude). The dynamic imprint counts the whole
      39 m disk, untapered, at the worst of four sampled incidence sides (others are not computed), and beyond it the scattered
      wave by energy-flux conservation for a surface wave without attenuation, to the scene’s farthest corner, with the grid
      check’s allowance. Under the lorry the margin rests on how far the scattered wave carries: counted within 39 m it is
      several times larger.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="depth">
    <h2>{orc.depth.residual_fraction > 0.3 ? `Depth is carried by the mark’s shape in this model: rooms spread 15 m down leave ${Math.round(100 * orc.depth.residual_fraction)}% of a deeper room’s mark unmatched; the published method’s axis does not use it` : `Absolute depth is the hardest claim: rooms spread 15 m down match a deeper room’s mark to within ${(100 * orc.depth.residual_fraction).toFixed(1)}%`}</h2>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">any method · P2-29, P2-30</span>
        <h3>An obstruction sought, and not found</h3>
        <p>Could shallower voids mimic a deeper one? Small rooms 15 m down, spread over the ground in any non-negative mix, were
        fitted to the surface mark of the bench room 30 m down on the lab’s solver (two solved together agree with their sum to
        {' '}{(100 * orc.depth.pair_interaction_fraction).toFixed(1)}%): {Math.round(100 * orc.depth.residual_fraction)}% of the mark is left unmatched, and the bench room
        15 m down alone leaves {Math.round(100 * orc.depth.best_single_bench_room_residual)}%. In uniform rock the mark changes shape with depth, not only size, so telling these
        depths apart takes only {orc.depth.signal_factor_depth_over_detection.toFixed(1)} times the signal detection takes.
        {fc.depth_oracle && ` Theorem E's floor on any method's mean depth error, where detection just reaches 95% at 5%, is then only ${fc.depth_oracle.rows[0].mean_depth_error_lower_m.toFixed(1)} m of their ${fc.depth_oracle.separation_m} m separation.`}
        {' '}Richer classes (layered rock, fractures, voids of other shapes) could still mimic one another; that is not tested.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">the published method · P2-28</span>
        <h3>Its depth follows the shaking, not the room</h3>
        <p>Run whole over the bench room 15 m down, the same room 30 m down and a granite block, each imprint boosted to 2 rad, the
        published focusing spreads each change over its whole axis: {pctp(d15.share_12_18_m, 0)} of the 15 m room’s change lies between 12 and 18 m
        and {pctp(d30.share_27_33_m, 0)} of the 30 m room’s between 27 and 33 m, where {pctp(d15.axis_share_12_18_m, 0)} and {pctp(d30.axis_share_27_33_m, 0)} of the axis lie.
        Horizontally its change does gather over the room ({conc(d15).toFixed(1)} times elsewhere), but a random perturbation of the
        same pixels gathers almost as much ({conc(dNull).toFixed(1)} times): in this paired comparison the picture changes where the image
        changes. Its depth coordinate is an uncalibrated focus coordinate, the frequency of the registration series (P2-06): a calibration
        and aliasing problem, which a method that recovered the right layout would survive. It does not recover the layout: in
        the shape test (above) its blind map names a layout no better than guessing even where a reference detector names every one.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="open">
    <h2>What stays open: the physical envelopes, the open regime, and measurement</h2>
    <table class="data compact">
      <thead><tr><th>open</th><th>why</th><th>what would close or open it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Strong shaking close by, known exactly</td><td>a room under a 5 m roof with a lorry beside it all pass: the oracle’s upper bound {tvTxt(orThin?.tv_upper)}, {orOpen.length ? `${orOpen.length} cases not excluded after allowances (×${Math.round(orc.allowance_product)})` : 'every case excluded after allowances'}; the speckle bounds have no valid reference there yet, and the oracle’s margin rests on how far the scattered wave carries</td><td>a covariance floor for the ground moving with the lorry, or a calibrated noise floor; a physical limit on how far the scattered wave carries; a measurement over a known void</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The physical envelopes</td><td>the imprint is the lab’s linear solver in uniform bare rock (P2-04, P2-26); the ambient levels are regional; nonlinear or parametric coupling is not modelled</td><td>ground motion measured on the plateau during a pass; a proponent’s quantitative prediction of the room-specific motion</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The measurement model</td><td>speckle and the Doppler-to-time relation for the certificate and the tight layer; the radiometry for the oracle (30 dB assumed)</td><td>a qualified observation operator and noise floor for the real product</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Measured validation</td><td>the geophone-and-satellite comparison (P2-22, P2-23) is simulated</td><td>shaker or reflector data with instruments on them; surveyed voids and matched controls, blind</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="sub">
      A method that succeeds on well-designed, withheld ground would show that the measurements or these inputs deserve revision;
      the bounds are written so such a revision can be made, not to protect a conclusion. The test that would settle it is frozen
      and blind: motion recovered from a measured moving reflector first, then withheld cavities and controls predicted with
      settings fixed in advance (<a href={`${base}/satellite/#settle`}>What would settle it</a>). What would help most from the method’s
      proponents is a predicted room-specific surface motion, with its source, footprint, amplitude and phase, losses and duration,
      which can be put straight into these formulas without releasing any software.
    </p>
  </section>
</Doc>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLDetailsElement>('.calculator').forEach((box) => {
    const area = box.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('input[name="area"]')!;
    const disp = box.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('input[name="displacement"]')!;
    const floor = box.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('input[name="floor"]')!;
    const out = box.querySelector<HTMLOutputElement>('output')!;
    const lam = Number(box.dataset.lambda);
    const dens = Number(box.dataset.density);
    const calc = () => {
      const A = area.valueAsNumber;
      const u = disp.valueAsNumber * 1e-6;
      const z = floor.valueAsNumber;
      if (![A, u, z].every(Number.isFinite) || A <= 0 || u < 0 || z <= 0) {
        out.textContent = 'Enter a positive footprint and floor, and a motion of zero or more.';
        return;
      }
      const q = Math.sqrt((dens * A) / z) * ((4 * Math.PI * u) / lam);
      const rho = 2 * q + q * q;
      const kl = rho < 1 ? (rho * rho) / (2 * (1 - rho)) : Infinity;
      const v = Math.min(1, Math.sqrt(kl / 2), Math.sqrt(-Math.expm1(-kl)));
      out.textContent =
        v >= 0.9
          ? 'The certificate does not exclude 95% at 5% for these inputs: unresolved, which does not make detection possible.'
          : `Found ≤ false alarms + ${v.toPrecision(3)}: at 5% false alarms, at most ${(100 * Math.min(1, 0.05 + v)).toFixed(1)}%. ${v < 0.05 ? 'Within five points of chance.' : 'The 95% at 5% target is excluded; a detector could still find up to that much.'}`;
    };
    [area, disp, floor].forEach((el) => el.addEventListener('input', calc));
    calc();
  });
</script>

